The Undercroft is the lower city of [[Geography/Istria/Iomenei/index|Iomenei]]: hundreds of labyrinthian levels of metal decking, with no windows and lit by lumen light, magic lamps studded through the decks. The windows were left out because they would be structural weaknesses something could attack from outside. Tens of thousands of crofters live here, most of them born in the Undercroft and unlikely ever to see the sun's light. They repair the engines, synthesize the fuel, stow the cargo and keep the strider moving at all times.

Life below decks is work and sleep, and not much else. People have little money to spare, so the entertainment that exists trades in ration slips: bets on fighting rings, races between void rats around a little track, bars that swap homebrew swill for a ration card. Attempts to unionize are known to be fatal. If word of one reaches the upper decks, everyone involved is killed, and possibly their families too.

The fluid masters are the workers who handle the lubricants and fuels fed into the legs through the intake pipes.

The Mutant Den is a part of the Undercroft where the people who are not fit to work are pushed, and many mongrel folk end up there. Mongrel folk are mutants born of people who work around archaeotech, which can twist those born near it. They are not treated well, because the line between a harmless mutation and one worked by a malign force is thin, and some ancient devices can corrupt the people around them. Brayro, a worker in Strider Leg Armature Containment Unit 14, is a mongrel folk and once a dwarf: his torso has elongated and his spine bends him permanently to one side, so that he has two arms on his right and one on his left, and his right eye is three times the size it should be. His extra arm helps with the work.

A forsaken sector is a part of Iomenei that people are forbidden to enter, whether because an accident left it unlivable or because the technology inside was judged not worth the risk. Fighting rings are not quite legal, and they tend to set up near or inside forsaken sectors where there is little foot traffic. One such ring sits on a metal grate suspended from chains above a series of vats of caustic liquid, the predecessor components of the city's fuel. Fighters there go in unarmed, and the loser's body is pushed into the vats and dissolved. Ration slips change hands, and the bookie is [[People/Varian Khan|Varian Khan]].

One forsaken sector has been sealed and abandoned for about two hundred years. Whatever emptied it, the people left in a hurry: boxes and fabrics lie strewn through the halls, old dried blood is splattered on the ground, and short claw marks are scrabbled into the floor and walls. The warping in the floor and walls is worse than anywhere else in the city, the sign of a slip along the [[Phenomena/Infinite Horizon|Infinite Horizon]] that went badly wrong.

Just inside that sector is a workers' room with lockers where people once stored their things before going further in. Old ration slips rot there, of a style no longer used, and the lockers hold sacred unguents and magical seals of the kind used in rituals dealing with an [[Phenomena/Engine Heart|Engine Heart]], some with ingredients that do not match what is usually used with one. Past a sealed airlock door is a room about 500 feet across, built like a cathedral inside the city, its walls lined with stained glass frescoes and a player organ at the back whose wheel and armatures would drive air through organ pipes twisting through the room. The organ is a decorated [[Phenomena/Engine Heart|Engine Heart]], dead or out of commission. A hive of [[Bestiary/Vermin|swarm children]] nested in the room around their [[Bestiary/Vermin|Hivemother]], coiled on the pillar at its center. The hive and its Hivemother are dead.

There is no trace of [[People/Gerhard|Gerhard]] in the sector, which points to more of the insects having taken root somewhere else in the city.
